:"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"0de5f4210f5edfaf2b6fe64d464684e183c7a46b"},{"id":"q-dd410733197a","stem":"Read the following problem text: A 22 year old boy present with massive hepatospleenmegaly and originates from a low land area where rice is cultivated by irrigation. In addition to that white blood cells count reveal high eosinophilia and hemoglobin 8g/dl. Answer the following questions based on the above problem text","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Transfusion Medicine","topicOriginal":"Transfusion Medicine","subtopic":"Blood Grouping and Compatibility","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Which parasite or parasites may be responsible for this condition?"},{"label":"B","text":"What recto test will you perform to confirm your suspected diagnosis?"},{"label":"C","text":"If you do serology test and you find it positive, what is your interpretation and explain advantage and disadvantage of this serological test."},{"label":"D","text":"What tests you could do if the patient present with an acute infection"},{"label":"E","text":"How can you treat this condition?"},{"label":"F","text":"How will you prevent transmission of this condition in an irrigation scheme Section II :LONG ESSAYS: (30 marks)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Transfusion Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-798f6dbecc74","stem":"Discuss about Schistosoma spp host snails with particular reference to their biology and control","type":"Essay / Structured Essay","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Helminthology","topicOriginal":"Helminthology","subtopic":"Schistosomiasis","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Helminthology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3f4c9680d77a","stem":". Inner Light Sensitive Layer (Neural) - Retina. This sphere contains 3 different organs or fluids ( the contents of the eye)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". A jelly - The Vitreous Humour"},{"label":"B","text":". A solid - The Crystalline Lens"},{"label":"C","text":". A liquid - The Aqueous Humour. 3 [pic] Fig.2. Diagram of the anterior segment of the Eye. THE THREE COATS/LAYERS OF THE EYE I). The Outer Layer. The outer layer consists of 2 structures, the cornea & sclera."},{"label":"A","text":".The Cornea. All the light must enter the eye through the cornea ( The window of the eye). As well as letting the light into the eye, the cornea is also a powerful lens ( + 43), and as such refracts the light in such a manner, that a clear impression is formed on the retina. The cornea must be transparent , must refract light and must provide protection. The cornea is an evolutionary compromise, being a multicomponent, thick, tough, avascular tissue with a smooth surface and uniform curvature. The corneal transparency, its most important property, is due to a number of related factors: • regular & smooth epithelium; • lack of vessels; • regular arrangement of stromal elements; • state of hydration ( relative dehydration). The cornea consists of five layers:"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-216b71ea113e","stem":"The endothelium is a single layer of hexagonal cells lining the inner surface of the cornea. It contains metabolic pumps being chiefly responsible for keeping the cornea in a state of relative dehydration, thus ensuring its transparency. The endothelium does not regenerate; in the event of massive destruction of endothelial cells, corneal dehydration cannot be maintained; the cornea retains water ( pumps not functioning) and becomes oedematous (transparency compromised). [pic] Fig.3. Diagram representing the layers of the cornea. 5","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Water","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":".The Sclera. The sclera is a dense , white tissue, made of collagen. The white, opaque nature of the sclera, can be ascribed to : the irregular arrangement of collagen fibres and their high water content. The sclera, both protects the intraocular contents and maintains the shape of the globe. The sclera changes into the cornea, at the limbus. The sclera is covered externally, from the limbus by a mucous membrane called conjunctiva. The conjunctiva reflects at the posterior side of the sclera, and covers the internal side of the eyelids, fusing with the skin at the level of eyelid margin. It contains goblet cells ( mucin secretors), lymphoid , connective tissues, vessels and nerves. II). The middle Vascular Layer - THE UVEA ( UVEAL TRACT ) The iris, ciliary body and choroids, together form the uvea .There is no clear demarcation between these 3 parts, they are continuous with each other, lining the sclera from the anterior opening to the posterior aperture for the optic nerve."},{"label":"A","text":".The Iris is a ring of pigmented tissue. In the middle of the iris , there is an aperture called pupil, and all the light entering the eye , must pass through this opening. The iris, by contracting the sphincter muscle – parasympathetic innervation-, makes the pupil smaller ( miosis). Bright light makes the pupil contract ( small). Contraction of the iris the dilator muscle – sympathetic innervation -, enlarges the pupil ( mydriasis ). Dim light makes the pupil dilate ( large). The iris varies in colour from one individual to another, depending on the amount of pigment contained."},{"label":"B","text":".The ciliary body is the posterior continuation of the iris. It contains the ciliary muscle , responsible for the phenomenon of accommodation. The crystalline lens is suspended by the zonules to the ciliary body, and when the ciliary muscle contracts or relaxes, the lens changes its shape ( accommodates). It contains also the ciliary processes, these being responsible for the secretion of the aqueous humour. 6 Cholinergic and Adrenergic Neurotransmitters& Receptors in the Iris and Ciliary Body. The iris sphincter muscle and the ciliary body ciliary muscle, are innervated by cranial nerve III via parasympathetic fibres. The impulses are transmitted to the muscles by acetylcoline ( Ach). The neurons that synthesize , store and release Ach are called cholinergic neurons. The receptors receiving input from the cholinergic neurons are called cholinergic receptors. They are two types of cholinergic receptors: muscarinic and nicotinic. The dilator muscle of the iris has a sympathetic innervation and the impulses are transmitted to the muscle cells by norepinephrine (NE). The neurons that synthesize, store and release NE are called adrenergic neurons. The receptors receiving input from the adrenergic neurons are called adrenergic receptors. The adrenergic receptors are subdivided : • alpha 1; • alpha2; • beta1; • beta2. The iris dilator muscle has predominantly alpha receptors."},{"label":"C","text":".The Choroid, the most posterior part of the uvea, is a thin , pigmented, vascular layer situated between the sclera and the retina. The choroidal vessels supply with blood part of the retina (external part). There is a high flow of blood through the choroid, which means that the oxygen demands of the retina are very high, similar to that of the brain. 7 [pic] Fig.4. Details of the Uveal structure. III). The Inner Neural Layer - The Retina. The retina is the transparent, neural, innermost tissue of the eyeglobe. It has an outer part- the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), and an inner part the neurosensory retina. Structurally , they are 10 layers in the retina; the retinal pigment epithelium , is most external, and the photoreceptors/ light sensitive cells ( rods and cones) are imbedded in this pigmented layer. The rods are more numerous at the retinal periphery , and they are responsible for vision in reduced illumination. 8 The cones predominate in the central retina ( the fovea contains only cones) and they provide sharp central vision and the perception of colour. The cones and rods synapse with the so called bipolar cells, and these further synapse with the ganglion cells. The axons of the ganglion cells, gather together , and form the optic nerve. These axons long and fibre like course over the surface of the retina and converge at the optic disc ( papilla), where the nerve exists the globe. [pic] Fig.5. Diagram of the histological layers of the human retina. Retinal topography. On the nasal side of each retina, there is a pink-yellow oval to round structure called optic disc / optic nerve head /papilla. It is made up by the axons of the ganglion cells leaving the retina. 9 The centre of the disc appears whiter than the rest , it is called optic cup; it is the site where the retinal vessels enter ( arteries) and leave ( veins) the eyeglobe. Temporal to the disc is the macula. This is the most posterior part of the globe and the central part of the retina. It is situated between the temporal vascular arcade, it contains mainly cones. The centre of the macula is an area called fovea centralis. The fovea appears yellowish ( due to a pigment – xantophyll), it contains only cones and it is responsible for finely detailed central vision and colour vision. [pic] Fig. 6. The macular area and the optic disc. Retinal biochemistry and physiology. Catching light and converting its energy into a neural response distinguishes the retina from all other neural structures , which it otherwise resembles. In most nerve cells, transient depolarisation generates an action potential ; in photoreceptors the response is graded ( the changes in membrane polarisation being proportional to the amount of stimulating light). The conversion of light energy stored in a single photon to an electrochemical response , is called PHOTOTRANSDUCTION. It is also called , phototransduction cascade, because it consists of a cascade of reactions, one triggering the other. The phototransduction cascade in the rod , begins with the activation of rhodopsin ( the visual pigment of the rod) , which then activates transducin , which activates phosphodiesterase (PDE). 10 PDE hydrolyses cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP), whish leads to the closure of Na and Ca ( cation) channels. Closure of the cation channels is due to the decrease in cGMP levels. The cation channels being closed, the cell membranes hyperpolarize ( change in memrane potential), and neurotransmitter release is reduced. The Ca channels being closed, there is a reduction of intracellular Ca; this stimulates guanylate cyclase (GC), to synthesize more cGMP. The increased cGMP levels open the cation channels ; as a result the photoreceptor cells become depolarised, and , the neurotransmitter glutamate is released from the synaptic terminal, starting the neural signals for vision. These neural signals started in the photoreceptor cells, are transmitted to the bipolar cells, then to the ganglion cells. Via the ganglion cell axons ( which form the optic nerve) and the rest of the visual pathways, the signal is transmitted to the visual (occipital ) cortex, where the information is analysed and the sensation of vision takes place. Cone system and colour vision. The cone photoreceptors more numerous in the central retina and are responsible for fine, detailed and colour vision. Cones have 3 different opsins ( visual pigments) that selectively confer sensitivity to red, green and blue light. Cone phototransduction resembles that of the rods, but it’s faster and stronger. Because of their ability to adapt, cones are indispensable to good vision. Without cones , one is legally blind , losing the ability to read and see colours. Lost rod function is a minor handicap. THE CONTENTS OF THE EYE"},{"label":"A","text":". Vitreous humour. The vitreous is a clear jelly-like substance that fills the intraocular cavity behind the lens. Acts as a shock absorber and maintains the spherical shape of the globe. Normally, the vitreous gel is optically transparent, so that light can pass freely to focus on the retina. 11"},{"label":"B","text":". The Crystalline Lens. [pic] Fig. 7. Diagram of the human Crystalline Lens. The crystalline lens is a transparent , biconvex, semisolid, avascular organ , located immediately behind the iris and the pupil. It comprises: • The capsule ( anterior and posterior); • The lens epithelium , under the anterior capsule . This is a single layer of active ,mitotic cells; • The cortex : made –up by lens fibers; • The nucleus: the innermost, hardest structural part of the lens. The lens is hold in position ( to the ciliary body), by the zonules ( zonules of Zinn ), fine fibres made –up by a noncollagenous material. When the ciliary muscle contracts, the zonules relax and the lens becomes more spherical – accommodates. By accomodating, the eye changes its focus from a distant to a near point allowing us to see clearly at distance ( 6 meters) and at near (33cm). Crystalline lens biochemistry. The lens’s protein content is 33%, the highest in the body. There are two major groups of proteins : crystallins ( water soluble) and water insoluble proteins There are three categories of Crystalline lens proteins : • alpha; • beta; • gamma. 12 Energy, in form of ATP, is produced in the lens primarily through anaerobic glycolysis . This is necessitated by the fact that oxygen is scarce in the lens and its surroundings. It has long been known that lenses placed in organ culture function quite well in the total absence of oxygen as long as glucose is provided. Removing glucose from the medium , however, causes rapid impairment of lens metabolic activity and loss of transparency , in the presence or absence of oxygen. The lens lacks innervation and has no vascular system; it depends totally on the aqueous and vitreous for its nourishment. Physiologic aspects. The human lens grows continuously throughout life and retains within it all the cells formed during its lifetime. No cells are shed. Because of its avascularity and its mode of growth, the lens faces some unusual problems. All nutrients must be contained from the surrounding fluids; likewise , all waste products must be released into those fluids. These characteristics make the lens unique and remarkable for its ability to preserve its specialised function of transparency throughout the human life span."},{"label":"C","text":". The Aqueous Humor The aqueous humour fills the anterior chamber. The fluid and electrolyte composition is similar to that of the plasma. It is produced then secreted by the ciliary body epithelium into the posterior chamber; from the posterior chamber passes through the pupil into the anterior chamber. Here circulates between the iris, lens and cornea, then slowly drains through the trabecualar meshwork. From the trabecular meshwork , the aqueous enters the canal of Schlemm and from here it drains into the subconjunctival veins. The aqueous, by circulating in the anterior chamber, provides oxygen and nutrients for the lens and corneal endothelium. The pressure produced by the continual production of aqueous is the intraocular pressure ( IOP). Normal IOP values are between"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-810e059d1bc4","stem":"When a person looks at an object , light rays are reflected from that object to the eye.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c41136857046","stem":"which means that the oxygen demands of the retina are very high, similar to that of the brain.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3b6e6f6130f8","stem":"which it otherwise resembles.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-589c9934a4c9","stem":"When the ciliary muscle contracts, the zonules relax and the lens becomes more spherical – accommodates. By accomodating, the eye","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-20bd682da879","stem":"When the eye is stimulated by light, the result is the light reflex : constriction of the pupil ( miosis).","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5593c609c3bc","stem":"When one eye is stimulated by light , the fellow eye simultaneously and equally constricts.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c1ed015c6efe","stem":"When RAPD is present, the pupil from the abnormal eye will dilate instead of constricting.This is a paradoxical reaction ,and it","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-04712dc01d46","stem":"Acute pancreatitis may be cause by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascaris lumbricoids"},{"label":"B","text":"Entorboius vermicularis"},{"label":"C","text":"Ancylostoma duodenale"},{"label":"D","text":"Trichuris trichiura"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-599ca6a9aff3","stem":"All of the followings are parasites may lead by away or another to diarrhea except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"trichuris trichiura"},{"label":"B","text":"hook worms"},{"label":"C","text":"fasciolopsis buski"},{"label":"D","text":"strongyloids stercoralis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7213b9aab548","stem":"through consumption of infected raw vegetables , all of the followings are parasites acquired to man except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"hymenolepis nana"},{"label":"B","text":"toxocara canis"},{"label":"C","text":"trichuris trichiura"},{"label":"D","text":"trichinella spiralis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Scien